{
  "topic": "Why Earth has seasons",
  "language": "en",
  "category": "ciencia",
  "title": "Why do we have summer and winter? 🌍☀️",
  "hook": "Why do summer and winter exist?",
  "presenter": "perro",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "role": "hook",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_hook",
      "narration": "Why do summer and winter exist?",
      "on_screen_text": "Why are there seasons?",
      "highlight_words": [
        "seasons?"
      ],
      "sfx": "impact",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_distance",
      "narration": "Many people think it's because we get closer to the Sun. But Earth is closest in January, when it's winter in the north!",
      "on_screen_text": "Closest in January!",
      "highlight_words": [
        "January!"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_tilt",
      "narration": "The real secret is that Earth's axis is tilted about twenty-three degrees.",
      "on_screen_text": "Earth is tilted",
      "highlight_words": [
        "tilted"
      ],
      "sfx": "riser",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_june",
      "narration": "In June, the northern hemisphere leans toward the Sun: it gets more direct light and longer days. It's summer.",
      "on_screen_text": "June: northern summer",
      "highlight_words": [
        "summer"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_rays",
      "narration": "A direct ray packs its heat into a small spot; a slanted one spreads the same heat over a much bigger area.",
      "on_screen_text": "Direct heats more",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Direct"
      ],
      "sfx": "pop",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_december",
      "narration": "Six months later it flips: the north leans away from the Sun and it's winter, while the south has summer.",
      "on_screen_text": "December: it flips",
      "highlight_words": [
        "flips"
      ],
      "sfx": "whoosh",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "role": "development",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_orbit",
      "narration": "And since the axis always points the same way, this repeats every year.",
      "on_screen_text": "It repeats every year",
      "highlight_words": [
        "year"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "role": "surprise",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_equator",
      "narration": "At the equator the Sun shines almost straight down all year. That's why Ecuador doesn't have four seasons, just a rainy season and a dry season.",
      "on_screen_text": "Ecuador: rainy and dry",
      "highlight_words": [
        "Ecuador:"
      ],
      "sfx": "ding",
      "transition": "cut"
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "role": "closing",
      "type": "scene3d",
      "model3d": "est_outro",
      "narration": "Send this to someone who thinks we're closer to the Sun in summer.",
      "on_screen_text": "Send this to a believer",
      "highlight_words": [
        "believer"
      ],
      "sfx": "none",
      "transition": "cut"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Seasons aren't caused by distance from the Sun (Earth is closest in early January) but by the roughly 23.4° tilt of its axis. When a hemisphere leans toward the Sun it gets more direct light and longer days. Near the equator the Sun is almost overhead all year. Original 3D animation; not to scale.",
